IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `scr-dates`
IMPORT `scr-duties`

-- Supreme Court (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025 (Vic) -- the PARTY's questions about documents, medical and expert
-- evidence and getting to trial: Order 29 (discovery), Order 32 (preliminary discovery and discovery from a non-party),
-- Order 33 (medical examination and exchange of hospital and medical reports), Order 44 (expert evidence), Order 48
-- (fixing a trial date) and Order 51 (assessment of damages).
--
-- SEAT: the individual who is a party to a civil proceeding in the Supreme Court of Victoria, usually the plaintiff in a
-- personal injury claim: "Which documents must I discover, and by when?", "Can I find out whom to sue?", "Must I attend
-- the defendant's medical examination?", "By when must I serve my medical reports?", "Can I call a doctor whose report I
-- did not serve?", "Who assesses my damages?". The Court's orders, leave and findings are asked as facts.
--
-- ANSWERS as in scr-process.l4. The Court is taken not to have ordered the Christmas vacation counted (r 3.04(1)).
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Supreme Court
-- (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025, S.R. No. 85/2025, Version No. 003 (1 January 2026), not the authorised version.
-- (c) State of Victoria; published with the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be
-- reproduced except in accordance with the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026
-- (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Supreme Court civil procedure: documents, medical and expert evidence, trial and damages`

§§ `Order 29 -- discovery of documents`

-- r 29.01(1): "Except where the Rules of this Order otherwise provide, the Order applies only -- (a) to a proceeding
-- commenced by writ; and (b) to a proceeding in respect of which an order has been made under Rule 4.07(1)." r 29.01.1(1):
-- "Unless the Court otherwise orders, discovery ... is limited to the documents referred to in paragraph (3)." (3): "the
-- documents required to be discovered are any of the following documents of which the party giving discovery is, after a
-- reasonable search, aware at the time discovery is given -- (a) documents on which the party relies; (b) documents that
-- adversely affect the party's own case; (c) documents that adversely affect another party's case; (d) documents that
-- support another party's case." (4): "(a) if a party giving discovery reasonably believes that a document is already in
-- the possession of the party to which discovery is given, the party giving discovery is not required to discover that
-- document; (b) ... is not required to give discovery of additional copies". r 29.02(1): "all documents which are or have
-- been in that party's possession". r 29.01(2): "possession means possession, custody or power". Privileged documents are
-- discovered but their privilege claimed (r 29.04(1)(d)).
-- A Court order limiting or expanding discovery: NOTHING-kind *points outside the text* (the order).
@export Must I discover this document in my Supreme Court case (rr 29.01, 29.01.1, 29.02)? In a case begun by writ, standard discovery covers documents you have or had, and know of after a reasonable search, that you rely on, that hurt your case, or that help or hurt another party's case; not one you reasonably believe the other side already has, nor extra copies. A privileged document is listed, with the privilege claimed.
GIVEN `was my proceeding begun by writ or continued as if it were` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding was commenced by writ (or the Court ordered under rule 4.07 that it continue as if it had been)
      `has the Court limited or expanded discovery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made an order limiting, dispensing with or expanding discovery in the case (answer no if not)
      `do I have or have I had it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document is or has been in your possession, custody or power
      `am I aware of it after a reasonable search` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after a reasonable search, you are aware of the document when you give discovery
      `do I rely on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you rely on the document
      `does it hurt my case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it adversely affects your own case
      `does it hurt or help another party's case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it adversely affects, or supports, another party's case
      `do I reasonably believe they already have it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you reasonably believe the party you give discovery to already has the document
      `is it only an additional copy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is just another copy of a document you are already discovering
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I discover this document` MEANS
    IF (NOT `was my proceeding begun by writ or continued as if it were`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `has the Court limited or expanded discovery`
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE IF `do I reasonably believe they already have it` OR `is it only an additional copy`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE IF    `do I have or have I had it`
                     AND `am I aware of it after a reasonable search`
                     AND (`do I rely on it` OR `does it hurt my case` OR `does it hurt or help another party's case`)
                   THEN JUST TRUE
                   ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 29.02(3): "A notice for discovery served before the pleadings are closed shall be taken to have been served on the day
-- after the pleadings close." r 29.03: "A party upon whom a notice for discovery is served shall make discovery of
-- documents within 42 days after the later of -- (a) service of the notice; or (b) the day upon which the notice is taken
-- by virtue of Rule 29.02(3) to have been served."
GIVEN `the day the notice was served` IS A DATE, `the day the pleadings closed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 29.03 -- the day the time for discovery starts` `the day the notice was served` `the day the pleadings closed` MEANS
    IF `the day the notice was served` `is after` `the day the pleadings closed`
    THEN `the day the notice was served`
    ELSE `the date` 1 `days after` `the day the pleadings closed`

GIVEN `the day the notice was served` IS A DATE, `the day the pleadings closed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 29.03 -- the last day for discovery` `the day the notice was served` `the day the pleadings closed` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 42 `days after` (`r 29.03 -- the day the time for discovery starts` `the day the notice was served` `the day the pleadings closed`) FALSE) FALSE

@export By what day must I make discovery after a notice for discovery is served on me (rr 29.02(3), 29.03)? Within 42 days after service of the notice, or, if it was served before the pleadings closed, after the day following their close. Making discovery means filing and serving an affidavit of documents (r 1.13).
GIVEN `the day the notice was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice for discovery was served on you, as an ISO date
      `the day the pleadings closed` IS A DATE @desc The day the pleadings closed (the 30th day after the defence or the last pleading, r 14.08), as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I make discovery` MEANS
    `r 29.03 -- the last day for discovery` `the day the notice was served on me` `the day the pleadings closed`

@export Did I make discovery in time after the notice for discovery, or am I still in time (r 29.03)?
GIVEN `the day the notice was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice for discovery was served on you, as an ISO date
      `the day the pleadings closed` IS A DATE @desc The day the pleadings closed, as an ISO date
      `have I made discovery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have filed and served your affidavit of documents
      `the day I made it` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day you served it (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What became of my step`
`Did I make discovery in time` MEANS
    `what became of the step` `make discovery of documents` (`r 29.03 -- the day the time for discovery starts` `the day the notice was served on me` `the day the pleadings closed`) (`r 29.03 -- the last day for discovery` `the day the notice was served on me` `the day the pleadings closed`) `have I made discovery` `the day I made it` `today`

-- r 29.09(2): "A party on whom a notice to produce is served ... shall, within 7 days after that service, serve on the party
-- requiring production a notice appointing a time within 7 days after service of the notice under this paragraph when, and
-- a place where, the documents may be inspected."
@export If a notice to produce the documents in my affidavit of documents is served on me, by what day must I serve a notice appointing a time and place for inspection (r 29.09(2))? Within 7 days after service, appointing a time within 7 days after my notice.
GIVEN `the day the notice to produce was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice to produce was served on you, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I appoint a time for inspection` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 7 `days after` `the day the notice to produce was served` FALSE) FALSE

DECLARE `What a default notice can bring` IS ONE OF
    `still time to make discovery after the default notice`
    `the proceeding may be dismissed`
    `the defence may be struck out, and judgment in default of defence may follow`
    `nothing under rule 29.12.1, discovery made in time`

-- r 29.12.1(2): "If a party required to make discovery of documents fails to make discovery within the time limited ...,
-- the party entitled to the discovery may serve on that party a notice in Form 29D." (3): "If, within seven days after
-- service of a notice under paragraph (2), the party on whom the notice is served does not make discovery, the Court may
-- order -- (a) if the party required to make discovery is the plaintiff, that the proceeding be dismissed; (b) if ... a
-- defendant, that the defendant's defence, if any, be struck out." (5): a defendant whose defence is struck out is treated
-- as in default of defence. r 63.16.1: the party served "shall pay the costs of the notice unless the Court otherwise
-- orders".
@export A default notice (Form 29D) has been served on me for not making discovery in time: what can happen (rr 29.12.1, 63.16.1)? If I do not make discovery within 7 days after it is served, a plaintiff's case may be dismissed and a defendant's defence struck out; and I pay the costs of the notice unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `my side` IS A `My side in the discovery` @desc Whether you are the plaintiff or a defendant
      `the day the default notice was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the default notice was served on you, as an ISO date
      `have I made discovery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have since made discovery
      `the day I made it` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What a default notice can bring`
`What can follow the default notice` MEANS
    IF `have I made discovery` AND `the day I made it` `is on or before` `the last day`
    THEN `nothing under rule 29.12.1, discovery made in time`
    ELSE IF (NOT `have I made discovery`) AND `today` `is on or before` `the last day`
         THEN `still time to make discovery after the default notice`
         ELSE CONSIDER `my side`
              WHEN `the plaintiff in the discovery` THEN `the proceeding may be dismissed`
              WHEN `a defendant in the discovery` THEN `the defence may be struck out, and judgment in default of defence may follow`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 7 `days after` `the day the default notice was served` FALSE) FALSE

DECLARE `My side in the discovery` IS ONE OF
    `the plaintiff in the discovery`
    `a defendant in the discovery`

-- r 29.15: "A party who has made an affidavit of documents is under a continuing obligation to make discovery of documents
-- with respect to documents of which the party obtains possession after making the affidavit."
-- No affidavit made yet: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export After making my affidavit of documents, must I also discover documents that come into my hands later (r 29.15)? Yes: the obligation continues.
GIVEN `have I made an affidavit of documents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have made an affidavit of documents in the case
      `did I get the document after making it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document came into your possession, custody or power after you made the affidavit
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I discover documents I got later` MEANS
    IF `have I made an affidavit of documents` AND `did I get the document after making it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Order 32 -- preliminary discovery and discovery from a non-party`

-- r 32.03(1): "The Court may make an order under paragraph (2) where -- (a) an applicant, having made reasonable inquiries,
-- is unable to ascertain the description of a person sufficiently for the purpose of commencing a proceeding in the Court
-- against that person ...; and (b) it appears that some person has or is likely to have knowledge of facts, or has, or is
-- likely to have or has had or is likely to have had in that person's possession any document or thing, tending to assist
-- in such ascertainment." (2): oral examination, and discovery of documents relating to the description. r 32.04: the same
-- for a party who wants to add a claim against a non-party. r 32.01: "description includes the name, place of residence,
-- place of business, occupation and sex of the person ... and whether that person is an individual or a corporation".
-- The conditions not met: JUST FALSE (a "where" condition; ruling (b)).
@export Can the Court order someone to help me find out who to sue (name, address, whether a company), by being examined or giving discovery (rr 32.03, 32.04)? Yes, if after reasonable inquiries I cannot identify the person well enough to sue, and someone appears to have, or likely has or had, knowledge, documents or things that would help.
GIVEN `have I made reasonable inquiries` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have made reasonable inquiries to identify the person you want to sue
      `can I still not describe them enough to sue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you still cannot ascertain the person's description (name, residence, business, occupation, whether an individual or a company) enough to start a proceeding
      `does someone appear to have helpful knowledge or documents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears that some person has (or likely has or had) knowledge of facts, or documents or things, that would help identify them
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get discovery to identify whom to sue` MEANS
    IF `have I made reasonable inquiries` AND `can I still not describe them enough to sue` AND `does someone appear to have helpful knowledge or documents`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 32.05: "Where -- (a) there is reasonable cause to believe that the applicant has or may have the right to obtain relief
-- in the Court from a person whose description the applicant has ascertained; (b) after making all reasonable inquiries,
-- the applicant has not sufficient information to enable the applicant to decide whether to commence a proceeding in the
-- Court to obtain that relief; and (c) there is reasonable cause to believe that that person has or is likely to have or
-- has had or is likely to have had in that person's possession any document relating to the question whether the
-- applicant has the right to obtain the relief and that inspection of the document by the applicant would assist the
-- applicant to make the decision -- the Court may order that that person shall make discovery to the applicant". r 32.06:
-- the same for a party wanting relief against a non-party.
-- The conditions not met: JUST FALSE (ruling (b)).
@export Before I decide whether to sue someone I have identified, can the Court order them to give me discovery of their documents (rr 32.05, 32.06)? Yes, if there is reasonable cause to believe I may have a claim against them, I still lack enough information after all reasonable inquiries to decide, and they likely have documents about it that would help me decide.
GIVEN `is there reasonable cause to believe I may have a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is reasonable cause to believe you have or may have a right to relief from the identified person (a judgment the Court makes)
      `after all reasonable inquiries do I still lack the information to decide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after all reasonable inquiries, you do not have enough information to decide whether to sue
      `are they likely to have documents that would help me decide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is reasonable cause to believe the person has (or likely has or had) documents about your right to relief whose inspection would help you decide
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get discovery from a prospective defendant` MEANS
    IF `is there reasonable cause to believe I may have a claim` AND `after all reasonable inquiries do I still lack the information to decide` AND `are they likely to have documents that would help me decide`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 32.07: "On the application of any party to a proceeding the Court may order that a person who is not a party and in
-- respect of whom it appears that that person has or is likely to have or has had or is likely to have had in that
-- person's possession any document which relates to any question in the proceeding shall make discovery to the applicant
-- of any such document."
-- Not a party, or no such appearance: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (subpoenas, Order 42, remain).
@export As a party to a case, can I get an order that someone who is not a party (a hospital, an employer, an insurer) give me discovery of their documents about a question in the case (r 32.07)?
GIVEN `am I a party to the proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the proceeding
      `does the non-party appear to have relevant documents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears that the non-party has (or likely has or had) documents relating to a question in the proceeding
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get discovery from a non-party` MEANS
    IF `am I a party to the proceeding` AND `does the non-party appear to have relevant documents`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- r 32.02: "An order made under this Order shall not operate to require the person against whom the order is made to
-- produce any document or answer any question which, on the ground of privilege, that person is not required to produce
-- or answer."
@export If an order for preliminary or non-party discovery is made against me, can it require me to produce a privileged document or answer a privileged question (r 32.02)? No.
GIVEN `is the document or question privileged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on the ground of privilege (for example legal professional privilege), you are not required to produce the document or answer the question
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the order make me produce it` MEANS
    IF `is the document or question privileged`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `Order 33 -- medical examination and hospital and medical reports`

-- r 33.01: "This Order applies to a proceeding in which the plaintiff claims damages for, or otherwise claims in respect of,
-- bodily injury." r 33.04(1): "The defendant may request the plaintiff in writing to submit to appropriate examinations by
-- a medical expert or experts at specified times and places." (2): "Where a plaintiff refuses or neglects, without
-- reasonable cause, to comply with a request under paragraph (1), the Court may, if the request was on reasonable terms,
-- stay the proceeding."
-- Not a bodily injury claim: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*. Otherwise the conditions not met: JUST FALSE
-- ("where" and "if").
@export Can my injury case be stayed if I do not attend the medical examination the defendant has asked for (rr 33.01, 33.04)? Yes, if the request was in writing and on reasonable terms and I refused or neglected without reasonable cause.
GIVEN `do I claim for bodily injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim damages for, or otherwise in respect of, bodily injury (including a mental condition or disease)
      `was the request in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant asked you in writing to attend an examination by a medical expert (doctor, dentist or psychologist) at a stated time and place
      `was the request on reasonable terms` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request was on reasonable terms (a judgment the Court makes)
      `have I refused or failed to attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have refused or neglected to comply
      `did I have reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had reasonable cause for not complying (for example illness)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my case be stayed for missing the medical examination` MEANS
    IF (NOT `do I claim for bodily injury`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `was the request in writing` AND `was the request on reasonable terms` AND `have I refused or failed to attend` AND (NOT `did I have reasonable cause`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 33.05(1): "The costs of and incidental to the examination shall be costs in the proceeding." (2): "the defendant, on
-- request by the plaintiff whether before or after the plaintiff is examined, shall pay to the plaintiff a reasonable sum
-- to meet the plaintiff's travelling and other expenses of, and incidental to, the examination."
-- No request made: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Must the defendant pay my travel and other expenses of attending its medical examination (r 33.05)? Yes, a reasonable sum, if I ask (before or after the examination).
GIVEN `do I claim for bodily injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim damages for, or otherwise in respect of, bodily injury
      `have I asked the defendant to pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the defendant to pay your travelling and other expenses of the examination
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the defendant pay my examination expenses` MEANS
    IF `do I claim for bodily injury` AND `have I asked the defendant to pay`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- r 33.06(1): "A defendant for whom a plaintiff is examined under Rule 33.04 shall as soon as practicable after the
-- examination obtain from the medical expert a medical report." (2): "On obtaining the medical report the defendant shall
-- forthwith serve the medical report on the plaintiff." (3): later reports from that expert too.
-- Not examined under r 33.04: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export After I am examined by the defendant's doctor, must the defendant get the doctor's report and serve it on me (r 33.06)? Yes: obtain it as soon as practicable, serve it on me straight away, and serve any later report from that doctor too.
GIVEN `was I examined at the defendant's request under rule 33.04` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were examined by a medical expert at the defendant's request under rule 33.04
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the defendant serve me its examiner's report` MEANS
    IF `was I examined at the defendant's request under rule 33.04`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `My side for the reports` IS ONE OF
    `the plaintiff serving reports`
    `a defendant or third party serving reports`

-- r 33.07(2): the plaintiff serves "(a) within 7 days after notice of trial under Order 48 is filed and served; and (b) in the
-- case of a hospital report or medical report made to or obtained by or for the plaintiff after the time referred to in
-- paragraph (a), forthwith -- or at such other time as the Court directs." r 33.08(3): the defendant (including a third or
-- subsequent party, (4)) "(a) within 14 days after notice of trial ...; and (b) ... after the time ..., forthwith". The
-- reports are those the party intends to tender, adduce or use at the trial ((1)).
-- A report obtained after that time ("forthwith", no day), or a time the Court directs: NOTHING-kind *points outside the
-- text*.
GIVEN `my side` IS A `My side for the reports`, `the day notice of trial was filed and served` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`rr 33.07, 33.08 -- the last day for the reports` `my side` `the day notice of trial was filed and served` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` `the days` `days after` `the day notice of trial was filed and served` FALSE) FALSE
    WHERE
        `the days` MEANS
            CONSIDER `my side`
            WHEN `the plaintiff serving reports` THEN 7
            WHEN `a defendant or third party serving reports` THEN 14

@export By what day must I serve the hospital and medical reports I intend to use at the trial of my injury case (rr 33.07, 33.08)? A plaintiff within 7 days, and a defendant within 14 days, after notice of trial is filed and served; a report obtained later must be served straight away; the Court may direct another time.
GIVEN `my side` IS A `My side for the reports` @desc Whether you are the plaintiff, or a defendant or third party
      `the day notice of trial was filed and served` IS A DATE @desc The day notice of trial under Order 48 was filed and served, as an ISO date
      `was the report obtained after that time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the report was made or obtained after the time for serving reports had passed (then it must be served forthwith)
      `has the Court directed another time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has directed another time for serving reports
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I serve my medical reports` MEANS
    IF `was the report obtained after that time` OR `has the Court directed another time`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`rr 33.07, 33.08 -- the last day for the reports` `my side` `the day notice of trial was filed and served`)

@export Did I serve my hospital and medical reports in time after notice of trial, or am I still in time (rr 33.07, 33.08)? Evidence from a medical expert that is not disclosed in a served report needs the Court's leave or the parties' consent (r 33.12).
GIVEN `my side` IS A `My side for the reports` @desc Whether you are the plaintiff, or a defendant or third party
      `the day notice of trial was filed and served` IS A DATE @desc The day notice of trial under Order 48 was filed and served, as an ISO date
      `have I served them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served the reports
      `the day I served them` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day you served them (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What became of my step`
`Did I serve my medical reports in time` MEANS
    `what became of the step` `serve the hospital and medical reports` `the day notice of trial was filed and served` (`rr 33.07, 33.08 -- the last day for the reports` `my side` `the day notice of trial was filed and served`) `have I served them` `the day I served them` `today`

-- r 33.08.1: where a medical expert whose report a party served makes another report to the party, "notwithstanding that
-- the party -- (a) no longer intends to tender the medical report so served ...; or (b) does not intend to tender the other
-- medical report ... -- the party shall serve the other medical report forthwith on each other party".
-- No served report from that expert: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export If a doctor whose report I served gives me a further report, must I serve it on the other parties even if I will not use it (r 33.08.1)? Yes, straight away.
GIVEN `did I serve an earlier report of this expert` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served a medical report of this medical expert under rule 33.07 or 33.08
      `has the expert made me another report` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the same expert has since made another medical report to you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I serve the expert's later report` MEANS
    IF `did I serve an earlier report of this expert` AND `has the expert made me another report`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- r 33.09: "Unless the Court otherwise orders, a party who is required to serve a copy of a hospital report or medical
-- report under this Order may exclude from the copy served -- (a) any expression of opinion in the original report on the
-- question of liability; and (b) ... any statement with respect to a fact that relates only to the question of liability."
@export May I leave out of the medical report I serve the doctor's opinion on who is liable, and facts that go only to liability (r 33.09)? Yes, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that the liability parts be included
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I leave out the liability opinion` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered otherwise`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 33.11(1): "This Rule does not apply in the case of the trial of a proceeding with a jury." (2): "A medical report which
-- was served under this Order is admissible as evidence of the opinion of the medical expert who gave the report and, where
-- the medical expert's oral evidence of a fact on which the opinion was based would be admissible, as evidence of that
-- fact." (3): it may be used by the party who served it or by a party on whom it was served. (4): tendered by the party
-- who served it: that party must make the expert attend for cross-examination "if notice that such attendance is required
-- is served ... not later than 5 days before the commencement of the trial", and if the expert does not attend "the Court
-- may order that the medical report be not received". (5): tendered by a party on whom it was served: that party must make
-- the expert attend.
-- A jury trial: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* ("does not apply").
@export At a trial without a jury, can a medical report served under Order 33 be received in evidence (r 33.11)? Yes, as evidence of the doctor's opinion and the facts it rests on, without the doctor if the party who served it tenders it and no one gave notice, at least 5 days before trial, requiring the doctor for cross-examination; where the doctor must attend (on such notice, or because the party tendering it was served with it) and does not, the Court may refuse it.
GIVEN `is the trial with a jury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the trial is with a jury
      `was the report served under Order 33` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the medical report was served under Order 33
      `did I serve it myself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you served the report (rather than it being served on you)
      `was attendance for cross-examination required in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you served it: whether another party served notice, not later than 5 days before the trial, requiring the doctor to attend for cross-examination; if it was served on you, answer yes (you must bring the doctor)
      `will the doctor attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor will attend the trial to be cross-examined
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the medical report be received in evidence` MEANS
    IF `is the trial with a jury` OR (NOT `was the report served under Order 33`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF ((NOT `did I serve it myself`) OR `was attendance for cross-examination required in time`) AND (NOT `will the doctor attend`)
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE JUST TRUE

@export By what day must I serve notice requiring the other side's doctor to attend the trial for cross-examination on their medical report (r 33.11(4)(a))? Not later than 5 days before the trial begins (weekends not counted).
GIVEN `the day the trial begins` IS A DATE @desc The day fixed for the commencement of the trial, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I require the doctor to attend` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day` 5 `days before` `the day the trial begins` FALSE) FALSE

-- r 33.10: in a trial without a jury, a party who intends to use a served report, or to call its maker, "shall file a copy
-- of the report for the use of the Court not less than 2 days before the day fixed for trial".
-- A jury trial: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* ("This Rule applies only to a proceeding which is to be tried
-- without a jury").
@export By what day must I file copies of the medical and hospital reports I will use for the Court, in a trial without a jury (r 33.10)? Not less than 2 days before the day fixed for trial (weekends not counted).
GIVEN `the day fixed for trial` IS A DATE @desc The day fixed for the trial, as an ISO date
      `is the trial with a jury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the trial is with a jury
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I file my medical reports for the Court` MEANS
    IF `is the trial with a jury`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day` 2 `days before` `the day fixed for trial` FALSE) FALSE)

-- r 33.12: "Except with the leave of the Court or by consent of the parties, a party shall not, except in cross-examination,
-- adduce evidence from a medical expert on medical matters concerning the plaintiff unless that evidence is disclosed by a
-- medical report served in accordance with this Order."
@export May I call evidence from a doctor, dentist or psychologist about my injuries that is not in a medical report I served (r 33.12)? Not without the Court's leave or the parties' consent (cross-examination aside).
GIVEN `is the evidence in a served report` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence is disclosed by a medical report served under Order 33
      `do I have leave or consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has given leave, or the parties consent
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I call undisclosed medical evidence` MEANS
    IF `is the evidence in a served report` OR `do I have leave or consent`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Order 44 -- expert evidence`

-- r 44.02(1): "This Order applies to a proceeding however commenced." (2): "This Order does not apply to the evidence of a
-- party who would, if called as a witness at the trial, be qualified to give evidence as an expert". (3): "With respect to
-- the opinion of a medical practitioner, in a proceeding for medical negligence in which the plaintiff claims damages for
-- or in respect of bodily injury, this Order applies to an opinion on the liability of the defendant but does not otherwise
-- apply to a medical report to which Order 33 applies." (Outside medical negligence, a medical report within Order 33 is
-- read as still within Order 44 too: fork F15.)
@export Does Order 44 (expert reports served 30 days before trial, the expert code of conduct) apply to this expert's evidence (r 44.02)? It applies in every proceeding, but not to a party who is an expert giving evidence; in a medical negligence injury claim, a doctor's opinion on liability is under Order 44 while the doctor's other medical reports are under Order 33.
GIVEN `is the expert a party to the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the expert is a party to the proceeding (giving evidence in their own case)
      `is it a medical negligence injury claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a proceeding for medical negligence in which the plaintiff claims damages for bodily injury
      `is the evidence a medical practitioner's opinion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence is the opinion of a medical practitioner (a doctor)
      `is it about the defendant's liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the opinion is on the liability of the defendant (for example whether the treatment fell below the standard)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does Order 44 apply to this expert evidence` MEANS
    IF `is the expert a party to the case`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `is it a medical negligence injury claim` AND `is the evidence a medical practitioner's opinion` AND (NOT `is it about the defendant's liability`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 44.03(1): "Unless otherwise ordered, a party who intends at trial to adduce the evidence of a person as an expert shall
-- -- (a) as soon as practicable after the engagement of the expert and before the expert makes a report under this Rule,
-- provide the expert with a copy of the code; and (b) not later than 30 days before the day fixed for trial, serve on each
-- other party, a report by the expert in accordance with clause 3 of the code and deliver a copy for the use of the Court."
-- The Court having ordered otherwise: NOTHING-kind *points outside the text*.
@export By what day must I serve my expert's report on the other parties (r 44.03(1)(b))? Not later than 30 days before the day fixed for trial, unless the Court orders otherwise; I must give the expert the code of conduct (Form 44A) before the report is made.
GIVEN `the day fixed for trial` IS A DATE @desc The day fixed for the trial, as an ISO date
      `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered a different time
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I serve my expert's report` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day` 30 `days before` `the day fixed for trial` FALSE) FALSE)

-- r 44.05: "Except with the leave of the Court or by consent of the parties affected, a party shall not, except in
-- cross-examination, adduce any evidence from a person as an expert at the trial of a proceeding unless the substance of
-- the evidence is contained within a report or reports which the party has served under this Order."
@export May I call expert evidence at trial whose substance is not in an expert report I served (r 44.05)? Not without the Court's leave or the consent of the parties affected (cross-examination aside).
GIVEN `is its substance in a report I served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the substance of the evidence is in an expert report you served under Order 44
      `do I have leave or consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has given leave, or the parties affected consent
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I call undisclosed expert evidence` MEANS
    IF `is its substance in a report I served` OR `do I have leave or consent`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 44.03(3): "If the expert provides to a party a supplementary report, including a report indicating that the expert has
-- changed the expert's opinion on a material matter ... (a) that party shall forthwith serve the supplementary report on all
-- other parties; and (b) in default of such service, the party and any other party having a like interest shall not use
-- the earlier report or the supplementary report at trial without the leave of the Court."
-- No supplementary report: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export My expert gave me a supplementary report (perhaps changing their opinion): if I have not served it, may I still use the earlier or the new report at trial (r 44.03(3))? Not without the Court's leave; it must be served on all other parties straight away.
GIVEN `has my expert given me a supplementary report` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your expert has given you a supplementary report
      `have I served it on all other parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served the supplementary report on all the other parties
      `do I have the Court's leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has given leave to use the reports anyway
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I use my expert's reports at trial` MEANS
    IF (NOT `has my expert given me a supplementary report`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `have I served it on all other parties` OR `do I have the Court's leave`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 44.04: "Unless otherwise ordered, a party may put in evidence a report served on that party by another party under
-- this Order."
@export May I put in evidence an expert report the other side served on me (r 44.04)? Yes, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that you may not
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I use the other side's expert report` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered otherwise`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 44.06(5): "Subject to paragraph (1)(b), except as the parties affected agree in writing, no evidence shall be admitted
-- of anything said or done by any person at the conference." (6): "An agreement reached during the conference shall not
-- bind a party except in so far as the party agrees in writing." (1)(b): the joint report to the Court.
@export Am I bound by an agreement the experts reached at a court-directed conference of experts (r 44.06(6))? Only so far as I agree in writing. (What is said there is not evidence unless the parties agree in writing, apart from the joint report, r 44.06(5).)
GIVEN `have I agreed to it in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have agreed in writing to what the experts agreed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I bound by the experts' conference agreement` MEANS
    IF `have I agreed to it in writing`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Order 48 -- fixing a date for trial`

DECLARE `Step after notice of trial` IS ONE OF
    `amend a pleading`
    `seek particulars`
    `seek answers to interrogatories`
    `seek discovery or inspection of documents`
    `give particulars of my damages in an injury claim`
    `serve medical reports or attend a medical examination`
    `serve an offer of compromise`

DECLARE `Special list` IS ONE OF
    `no special list`
    `the Admiralty, TEC, a Commercial, Corporations, Intellectual Property or Valuation, Compensation and Planning List`

-- r 48.01(1): the Order applies to a proceeding commenced by writ, originating motion, notice of appeal or application for
-- leave to appeal; (2): not in the Admiralty, TEC, Commercial, Corporations, Intellectual Property or Valuation,
-- Compensation and Planning Lists. r 48.05(1): after notice of trial has been filed (by the party filing it) or served (on
-- the party served), that party "shall not seek -- (c) amendment to a pleading; (d) particulars or further particulars; (e)
-- answers to interrogatories or further answers; or (f) discovery or inspection of documents or further discovery or
-- inspection -- without the leave of the Court." (2): this does not "(a) in a proceeding for damages for or arising out of
-- death or bodily injury affect the obligation of the plaintiff to give particulars of the damages claimed or the
-- obligation of the parties under Order 33 ...; (b) prevent the service of an offer in writing in accordance with Part 2 of
-- Order 26".
-- A special list, or before notice of trial: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export After notice of trial has been filed or served, may I still take this step without the Court's leave (r 48.05)? Amending a pleading, seeking particulars, interrogatories or more discovery needs leave; giving particulars of damages and medical reports in an injury claim, and serving an offer of compromise, do not.
GIVEN `which list` IS A `Special list` @desc Whether the case is in one of the special lists Order 48 does not apply to (Admiralty, TEC, Commercial, Corporations, Intellectual Property, Valuation Compensation and Planning), or none
      `have I filed or been served with notice of trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have filed notice of trial, or it has been served on you
      `which step` IS A `Step after notice of trial` @desc The step you want to take
      `is it a death or injury damages claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding is for damages for or arising out of death or bodily injury
      `do I have the Court's leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has given leave for the step
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I take this step after notice of trial` MEANS
    CONSIDER `which list`
    WHEN `the Admiralty, TEC, a Commercial, Corporations, Intellectual Property or Valuation, Compensation and Planning List` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `no special list` THEN
        IF (NOT `have I filed or been served with notice of trial`)
        THEN NOTHING
        ELSE CONSIDER `which step`
             WHEN `serve an offer of compromise` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `give particulars of my damages in an injury claim` THEN `the injury claim exception`
             WHEN `serve medical reports or attend a medical examination` THEN `the injury claim exception`
             OTHERWISE `with leave only`
    WHERE
        `with leave only` MEANS
            IF `do I have the Court's leave`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE JUST FALSE
        `the injury claim exception` MEANS
            IF `is it a death or injury damages claim`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE `with leave only`

-- r 48.07(4): "Except as all the parties who attend the conference agree in writing, no evidence shall be admitted of
-- anything said or done by any person at the conference." (5): the agreement may be made at the conference or later.
@export Can what I said or did at a pre-trial conference be used in evidence against me (r 48.07(4))? No, unless all the parties who attended agree in writing.
GIVEN `have all the attending parties agreed in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties who attended the pre-trial conference have agreed in writing that it may be admitted
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the pre-trial conference be used in evidence` MEANS
    IF `have all the attending parties agreed in writing`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Order 51 -- assessment of damages`

DECLARE `Who assesses my damages` IS ONE OF
    `an Associate Judge`
    `the judge at the trial against the other defendants`
    `as the Court has ordered`

-- r 51.01: "Subject to Rule 51.05, damages under any judgment or order for damages to be assessed shall, unless the Court
-- otherwise orders, be assessed by an Associate Judge." r 51.05: "Where judgment for damages to be assessed is entered or
-- given in default of appearance or pleading, and the proceeding is continued against other defendants, the damages shall
-- be assessed at the trial, unless the Court otherwise orders." (r 21.03(3) and r 22.09 send default and summary
-- judgments for damages to be assessed here.)
@export After I get judgment for damages to be assessed (for example by default or summary judgment), who assesses my damages (rr 51.01, 51.05, 21.03(3), 22.09)? An Associate Judge; but after a default judgment where the case goes on against other defendants, the judge at that trial, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `has the Court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered how or by whom the damages are to be assessed
      `was it a default judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment for damages to be assessed was entered or given in default of appearance or pleading
      `does the case go on against other defendants` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding is continuing against other defendants
GIVETH A `Who assesses my damages`
`Who will assess my damages` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered otherwise`
    THEN `as the Court has ordered`
    ELSE IF `was it a default judgment` AND `does the case go on against other defendants`
         THEN `the judge at the trial against the other defendants`
         ELSE `an Associate Judge`

-- r 51.02(2): "The party for whom the damages are to be assessed shall, not less than 10 days before the assessment is due,
-- serve notice of the day, time and place of the assessment on the other party to the assessment." (1): the other party
-- may take part.
@export By what day must I serve notice of the day, time and place of the assessment of my damages on the other party (r 51.02)? Not less than 10 days before the assessment; the other party may take part in it.
GIVEN `the day of the assessment` IS A DATE @desc The day the assessment of damages is due, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I give notice of the assessment` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day` 10 `days before` `the day of the assessment` FALSE) FALSE
